### What does findAny return and why is it an Optional?

When you search a stream for a matching element, the match might not exist. The HR department could be empty, or every employee could be in another department. Java models this possibly-absent result with `Optional<Employee>` instead of returning `null`, so the caller is forced to think about the empty case.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;

public class EmployeeFinder {
    public Optional<Employee> findAnyFromHr(List<Employee> employees) {
        return employees.stream()
                .filter(e -> e.getDepartment().equals("HR"))
                .findAny();
    }
}
```

The return type `Optional<Employee>` tells every caller: this method may give you an employee, or it may give you nothing, and you must decide what to do in each case.

### How do you define the Employee model?

The employee is a plain model class. Salary uses `BigDecimal` because it represents money.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class Employee {
    private final String name;
    private final String department;
    private final BigDecimal salary;

    public Employee(String name, String department, BigDecimal salary) {
        this.name = name;
        this.department = department;
        this.salary = salary;
    }

    public String getName() { return name; }
    public String getDepartment() { return department; }
    public BigDecimal getSalary() { return salary; }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return name + " (" + department + ")";
    }
}
```

### How do orElse, orElseGet, and orElseThrow unwrap the Optional?

Once you hold an `Optional<Employee>`, you need a value out of it. The three methods differ in what happens when the Optional is empty.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;

public class UnwrapDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> staff = List.of(
                new Employee("Rajesh", "Engineering", new BigDecimal("120000")),
                new Employee("Priya", "HR", new BigDecimal("95000"))
        );

        EmployeeFinder finder = new EmployeeFinder();

        // 1. orElse: return a ready default when empty
        Employee a = finder.findAnyFromHr(staff)
                .orElse(new Employee("Nobody", "HR", BigDecimal.ZERO));

        // 2. orElseGet: build the default lazily only if empty
        Employee b = finder.findAnyFromHr(staff)
                .orElseGet(() -> new Employee("Nobody", "HR", BigDecimal.ZERO));

        // 3. orElseThrow: treat absence as an error
        Employee c = finder.findAnyFromHr(staff)
                .orElseThrow(() -> new IllegalStateException("No HR employee found"));

        System.out.println(a);
        System.out.println(b);
        System.out.println(c);
    }
}
```

```text
Priya (HR)
Priya (HR)
Priya (HR)
```

### What is the real difference between orElse and orElseGet?

This is the most common Optional interview question. `orElse(x)` always evaluates `x`, even when the Optional has a value and the fallback is never used. `orElseGet(supplier)` evaluates the supplier only when the Optional is empty. When the fallback is cheap this makes no difference, but when the fallback hits a database or creates an object, the eager version does needless work.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.Optional;

public class EagerVsLazy {
    static String expensiveDefault() {
        System.out.println("expensiveDefault() was called");
        return "DEFAULT";
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Optional<String> present = Optional.of("Priya");

        System.out.println("-- orElse --");
        String a = present.orElse(expensiveDefault()); // runs even though value is present
        System.out.println("result = " + a);

        System.out.println("-- orElseGet --");
        String b = present.orElseGet(EagerVsLazy::expensiveDefault); // skipped, value present
        System.out.println("result = " + b);
    }
}
```

```text
-- orElse --
expensiveDefault() was called
result = Priya
-- orElseGet --
result = Priya
```

Notice the message prints under `orElse` even though the value is present, but not under `orElseGet`.

### How do orElse and orElseGet compare at a glance?

| Aspect | orElse(value) | orElseGet(supplier) |
|--------|---------------|---------------------|
| Argument type | a ready value | a `Supplier<T>` |
| When the fallback runs | always | only when empty |
| Cost if value present | evaluates fallback anyway | zero extra work |
| Best for | cheap constants | expensive or side-effecting defaults |
| Example | `opt.orElse("")` | `opt.orElseGet(() -> loadFromDb())` |

The rule: use `orElse` for a plain constant, and `orElseGet` whenever building the default costs anything.

### What does orElseThrow do and which form should you use?

`orElseThrow()` with no argument throws `NoSuchElementException`. `orElseThrow(supplier)` throws whatever exception you build, which lets you raise a meaningful domain exception that the rest of the system understands.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;

public class ThrowDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> noHr = List.of(); // empty: no HR employee
        Optional<Employee> result = noHr.stream()
                .filter(e -> e.getDepartment().equals("HR"))
                .findAny();

        try {
            Employee e = result.orElseThrow(
                    () -> new IllegalStateException("HR department has no staff"));
            System.out.println(e);
        } catch (IllegalStateException ex) {
            System.out.println("Caught: " + ex.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Caught: HR department has no staff
```

### What are common mistakes with Optional?

The worst mistake is calling `get()` without checking presence, which defeats the whole purpose of Optional and throws on empty.

```java
// WRONG: get() throws NoSuchElementException when empty
Employee e = finder.findAnyFromHr(staff).get();

// WRONG: checking isPresent then get is verbose and loses the benefit
Optional<Employee> opt = finder.findAnyFromHr(staff);
if (opt.isPresent()) {
    Employee e2 = opt.get();
    process(e2);
}
```

```java
// CORRECT: hand the value straight to a consumer, no manual check
finder.findAnyFromHr(staff)
        .ifPresent(this::process);

// CORRECT: provide a fallback inline
Employee safe = finder.findAnyFromHr(staff)
        .orElseThrow(() -> new IllegalStateException("No HR employee"));
```

The correct versions never risk an exception and read as a single statement.

### How is findAny different from findFirst?

Both return an `Optional`, but `findFirst` always returns the first element in encounter order, while `findAny` may return any matching element. On a sequential stream they usually behave the same, but on a parallel stream `findAny` can be much faster because it does not force ordering.

| Method | Returns | Ordering guarantee | Parallel performance |
|--------|---------|--------------------|-----------------------|
| findFirst() | first matching element | deterministic, encounter order | may be slower |
| findAny() | any matching element | none | often faster |

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;

public class FindAnyVsFirst {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> staff = List.of(
                new Employee("Priya", "HR", new BigDecimal("95000")),
                new Employee("Anita", "HR", new BigDecimal("98000"))
        );

        Optional<Employee> first = staff.stream()
                .filter(e -> e.getDepartment().equals("HR"))
                .findFirst();

        Optional<Employee> any = staff.parallelStream()
                .filter(e -> e.getDepartment().equals("HR"))
                .findAny();

        System.out.println("findFirst = " + first.orElse(null));
        System.out.println("findAny   = " + any.orElse(null));
    }
}
```

```text
findFirst = Priya (HR)
findAny   = Priya (HR)
```

Use `findFirst` when order matters, and `findAny` when any match will do and you want parallel speed.

### How do you transform the value inside an Optional?

You often do not need the whole employee, only one field. `map` transforms the value if present and stays empty otherwise, so you never touch a null.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class MapDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> staff = List.of(
                new Employee("Priya", "HR", new BigDecimal("95000"))
        );

        String name = staff.stream()
                .filter(e -> e.getDepartment().equals("HR"))
                .findAny()
                .map(Employee::getName)
                .orElse("UNKNOWN");

        System.out.println("HR contact: " + name);
    }
}
```

```text
HR contact: Priya
```

### What are the edge cases to watch for?

An Optional can wrap a value, but it should never itself be null, and it should not wrap another Optional. Returning `null` where an `Optional` is expected breaks every caller.

```java
// WRONG: returning null instead of an empty Optional
public Optional<Employee> broken(List<Employee> list) {
    if (list.isEmpty()) {
        return null; // callers will NPE on .orElse(...)
    }
    return list.stream().findAny();
}

// CORRECT: return Optional.empty(), never null
public Optional<Employee> safe(List<Employee> list) {
    return list.stream()
            .filter(e -> e.getDepartment().equals("HR"))
            .findAny();
}
```

Also note `Optional.of(x)` throws if `x` is null, while `Optional.ofNullable(x)` returns empty for null. Use `ofNullable` when the source might be null.

### When should you use Optional and when should you avoid it?

Use Optional as the return type of a method whose result may legitimately be absent, like a lookup. Do not use it as a field, a constructor parameter, or a method argument, because it adds overhead and complicates serialization without buying clarity.

```java
// GOOD: Optional as a return type for a lookup
public Optional<Employee> findManager(String department) { ... }

// AVOID: Optional as a field
public class Account {
    private Optional<String> nickname; // use a plain nullable String instead
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers often ask you to contrast `orElse` and `orElseGet` with a cost argument, explain why `get()` is discouraged, and show `ifPresentOrElse` from Java 9 which handles both branches at once.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class IfPresentOrElseDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> staff = List.of(
                new Employee("Priya", "HR", new BigDecimal("95000"))
        );

        staff.stream()
                .filter(e -> e.getDepartment().equals("HR"))
                .findAny()
                .ifPresentOrElse(
                        e -> System.out.println("Found " + e.getName()),
                        () -> System.out.println("No HR employee on record"));
    }
}
```

```text
Found Priya
```
